<section id="part-a-4"><h3>A.4: Blend the Images Into a Mosaic</h3>
<p>For each pair, I warp the left photo into the right photo’s coordinate system with bilinear interpolation. I compute a shared canvas that contains both images, then use the warped image’s offset to place it and its validity mask. The right image stays unwarped.</p>
<p>I use the distance transform of each mask to set blending weights. Each mask has a one-pixel false border before the distance calculation, so the canvas edges count as image boundaries. A pixel farther from an image boundary receives more weight. If only one image covers a pixel, that image receives full weight.</p>
<pre aria-label="Distance-weighted blending equation"><code>w_left  = d_left  / (d_left + d_right)
w_right = d_right / (d_left + d_right)

mosaic = w_left · left + w_right · right</code></pre>
<p>Where both distances are zero, I leave the output black. The masks record valid image coverage separately from pixel color, so valid black pixels still contribute to the blend.</p>
