<h2 class="report-part" id="part-b">Part B: Feature Matching for Autostitching</h2>
<p>In Part A, I clicked corresponding points by hand to tell the program which locations showed the same scene detail. In Part B, the program finds those correspondences automatically. It first chooses candidate points in each image, describes the appearance around each point, compares those descriptions across images, and uses RANSAC to select matches that agree on a homography. That homography then goes through the warping and blending code from Part A.</p>
<p>I used the same bedroom, kitchen, and outdoors image pairs. I resized each photo to 900 × 1200 pixels to reduce the cost of feature detection and matching.</p>
<section id="part-b-1"><h3>B.1: Harris Corner Detection</h3>
<p>A useful feature point has a recognizable neighborhood that can be found again in the other photo. Harris corner detection looks for locations where image intensity changes in two directions. Moving a small window across a flat wall changes little; moving along a straight edge can also change little. At a corner, movement in either direction changes the window’s appearance. This makes corners useful candidates for matching.</p>
<p>I used the supplied Harris detector at a single scale on grayscale images. It computes a corner response at each pixel, then returns local maxima and excludes points within 20 pixels of the image boundary. These are candidate locations only: detecting a point in the left image does not yet identify its partner in the right image. The red overlays below show that the initial detections are dense in textured areas.</p>
<p>Keeping every candidate would make later matching expensive. Keeping only the strongest responses could also concentrate points in a few textured regions. Adaptive non-maximal suppression (ANMS) favors strong points that have some space around them, so the selected features cover more of the image.</p>
<p>For each corner i, I find the distance to the nearest corner j whose response satisfies fᵢ &lt; 0.9fⱼ, where f is the Harris response. This distance is the corner’s suppression radius. A point close to a sufficiently stronger corner gets a small radius; an isolated strong point gets a large one. A point with no sufficiently stronger neighbor receives an infinite radius. I rank the radii and keep the largest 500 independently in each image.</p>
<p>The before-and-after plots show this reduction from roughly 25,000–27,000 candidates to 500 points per image.</p>
