<section id="part-b-2"><h3>B.2: Feature Descriptor Extraction</h3>
<p>ANMS gives me point locations. To compare a point in one photo with points in the other, I also need a description of what surrounds it. A feature descriptor stores that local appearance as a small array of numbers. Points that show the same scene detail should have similar descriptors, even though their image coordinates differ.</p>
<p>I blur each grayscale image with a Gaussian filter (σ = 2), then extract a 40 × 40 window around each selected corner. Sampling every fifth pixel near the center of each 5 × 5 block produces an axis-aligned 8 × 8 descriptor. The larger source window captures context around the corner; blurring before sampling reduces sensitivity to fine detail and small shifts. I keep the image axes fixed, so these descriptors do not compensate for rotation or changes in scale.</p>
<p>I flatten the 8 × 8 patch to 64 values, subtract its mean, and divide by its standard deviation. Subtracting the mean removes a uniform brightness offset. Dividing by the standard deviation adjusts the contrast scale. This helps corresponding patches compare well under simple brightness and contrast differences between photos.</p>
<pre aria-label="Descriptor normalization equation"><code>descriptor = (sampled patch − mean) / standard deviation</code></pre>
<p>The function skips windows that cross the image boundary and nearly constant patches, whose standard deviations are too small for reliable division. It keeps each descriptor paired with its point coordinates. All six images retained 500 descriptors, each with mean zero and standard deviation one.</p>
<p>Below, each top row shows four original 40 × 40 source windows; the bottom row shows their normalized 8 × 8 descriptors. Each column illustrates the conversion of one window into a descriptor.</p>
