<section id="part-b-3"><h3>B.3: Feature Matching</h3>
<p>For each of the 500 descriptors in the left image, I compare its 64 values with every descriptor in the right image. I subtract corresponding values, square the differences, and sum them. This sum of squared differences (SSD) is small when two patches have similar normalized appearance. The right-image descriptor with the smallest SSD is the nearest neighbor.</p>
<p>A nearest neighbor alone can be misleading: repeated patterns can give several candidates with similar scores. I therefore compare the best match with the second-best match. I accept the best candidate only when its SSD divided by the second-best candidate’s SSD is less than 0.5.</p>
<pre aria-label="Nearest-neighbor ratio test"><code>ratio = SSD to best candidate / SSD to second-best candidate
accept when ratio &lt; 0.5</code></pre>
<p>For example, SSD values of 10 and 40 give a ratio of 0.25, so I keep the best match. Values of 10 and 12 give about 0.83, so I reject it: two candidates look almost equally plausible. A smaller ratio indicates a more distinct best candidate.</p>
<p>The threshold applies to squared distances, so it corresponds to about 0.707 for ordinary Euclidean distances. RANSAC then checks which matches agree with a single homography.</p>
<p>Each line in the plots below connects one accepted left-image point to its proposed right-image partner. The number of lines is smaller than 500 because many descriptors fail the ratio test.</p>
