<section id="part-b-4"><h3>B.4: RANSAC for Robust Homography</h3>
<p>The ratio test gives proposed correspondences, including some incorrect ones. Fitting a homography to all of them at once could let those errors distort the result. RANSAC (Random Sample Consensus) searches for a homography that a large group of matches supports.</p>
<p>I implemented four-point RANSAC with 2,000 trials and a fixed random seed of 180. Each trial samples four matches without replacement and estimates a homography with <code>computeH</code>. Four point pairs provide the eight equations needed for the eight unknown homography entries, provided the points form a suitable configuration. The implementation skips samples with repeated destination points and estimates that are invalid or singular.</p>
<p>I test each candidate homography against every proposed match. I project the left-image point into the right image and measure the Euclidean distance to its matched right-image point. A match with an error below 3 pixels is an inlier for that candidate. The other matches are outliers for that candidate.</p>
<pre aria-label="RANSAC projection error"><code>predicted right point = H · left point, divided by its homogeneous coordinate
error = distance(predicted right point, matched right point)
inlier when error &lt; 3 pixels</code></pre>
<p>I keep the candidate with the largest inlier set. I then refit the homography using all matches in that set, which gives an overdetermined least-squares fit as in Part A, and recompute the inlier mask with the fitted homography. Repeating the random trials gives the algorithm chances to draw four consistent matches despite the outliers.</p>
<p>The table reports the final inlier counts and their mean projection errors after refitting. For example, the bedroom result retains 80 of the 122 proposed matches.</p>
<table><caption>RANSAC Results at 900 × 1200 Resolution</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Scene</th><th scope="col">Matches</th><th scope="col">Inliers</th><th scope="col">Mean Inlier Error</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><th scope="row">Bedroom</th><td>122</td><td>80</td><td>1.27 px</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">Kitchen</th><td>76</td><td>33</td><td>1.26 px</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">Outdoors</th><td>86</td><td>45</td><td>1.42 px</td></tr>
</tbody></table>
<p>I pass the estimated homography to <code>stitch_images</code>, which reuses Part A’s bilinear warping, shared canvas, and distance-weighted blending. It warps the left photo into the right photo’s coordinates, places both images on a canvas large enough to contain them, and blends their overlap. The automatic part is the selection of correspondences and estimation of the homography.</p>
<p>For each comparison below, the manual mosaic uses the clicked points from Part A, and the automatic mosaic uses the feature matches selected by RANSAC. Both use the same resized photos and blending code. I convert each original-resolution manual homography to the resized coordinate system with H_small = S H_original S⁻¹, where S scales pixel coordinates. This maps a resized source point back to original coordinates, applies the original homography, then scales the destination point down again.</p>
